        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0016</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0016/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils">(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="avatar">2</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>(H1). Every nilpotent pencil satisfies the incidence bound: the
dimension of the space of flags inscribed in the pencil is at most its
Riemann--Roch budget <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\chi (\mathcal {C}_{P})]]></fr:tex>, with equality controlled
by the adjoint torsion — for every type and multidegree, every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>,
every characteristic.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> A characteristic-free trace identity. Tangent vectors to
constant-type families are commutators over the function field, hence
lie in the adjoint torsion, hence are isotropic for the residue form
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex>; isotropy becomes a trace identity rather than a
genericity argument. The realized subspace is measured Lagrangian (the
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\delta ]]></fr:tex>-law) at every computed drop point.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Consequence.</html:strong> The nilpotent-pencil scheme is unconditionally a complete
intersection and the regular-class height bound holds for every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>, so
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">the height conjecture</fr:link> rides on the single statement
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link>. The elementary chain of the proof is formalized in
Lean 4 (formal/, no sorry up to the kernel-flatness hypothesis).</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0015/" title="Nilpotent pencils, drop points, the centralizer sheaf" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0015/" display-uri="ffi-0015" type="local">Nilpotent pencils, drop points, the centralizer sheaf</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 3; journal sec:h1; formal/ Lean
certificates.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0015/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0015</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0015/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Nilpotent pencils, drop points, the centralizer sheaf">Nilpotent pencils, drop points, the centralizer sheaf</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="avatar">2</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>A <html:em>nilpotent pencil</html:em> is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P = \mu  N + \nu  M]]></fr:tex>, a matrix
of linear forms over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B = K[\mu , \nu ]]]></fr:tex>, every member nilpotent. Its
generic Jordan type is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\tau ]]></fr:tex>, and a <html:em>drop point</html:em> is one of
the finitely many <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x \in  \mathbb {P}^1]]></fr:tex> at which the fiber type degenerates.</html:p>
            <html:p>The commutator map <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \operatorname {ad}_{P} : \mathfrak {gl}_n \otimes  \mathcal {O}
  \to  \mathfrak {gl}_n \otimes  \mathcal {O}(1)
]]></fr:tex>
on the pencil's <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {P}^1]]></fr:tex> has kernel the <html:em>centralizer sheaf</html:em>
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {C}_{P}]]></fr:tex>. The moving-flag count balances curve freedom against
inscribed-pencil freedom through the Riemann--Roch budget
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\chi (\mathcal {C}_{P})]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 3; journal sec:h1.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0014</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0014/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian">Orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="avatar">1</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Orbital varieties — the irreducible components of
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {O}_{\lambda } \cap  \mathfrak {n}]]></fr:tex> for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathfrak {n}]]></fr:tex> a fixed nilradical, Borel or
parabolic — are Lagrangian for the Kostant--Kirillov form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex> on
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {O}_{\lambda }]]></fr:tex>, for every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> and every characteristic.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Consequence.</html:strong> The <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-stable subvarieties of an orbit that the
height program must control sit exactly at half dimension, with the
isotropy form available as an identity rather than as a genericity
argument. This is avatar 1's mechanism theorem: it supplies the
symplectic half of every dimension bound in the program, and it is why
the surviving open content of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">the height conjecture</fr:link> is
purely <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0012/" title="The three recurring numbers" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0012/" display-uri="ffi-0012" type="local">The three recurring numbers</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0013/" title="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/" display-uri="ffi-0013" type="local">The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex sec:kk-lagrangian; journal pass 6--8
apparatus; machine censuses N13--N19 in experiments/pencil_ideal.sage.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0051/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0051</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0051/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The anomaly law">The anomaly law</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>conjecture</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="avatar">3</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">open</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>(anomaly law). Coisotropy of the survivor cocycle fails only at special
characteristic-2 germs.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> The law is the bookkeeping for why characteristic 2 is the sole
obstruction to characteristic-free statements at germ scale — which
germs are anomalous, and why only generic statements survive there.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Status.</html:strong> Open. Measured on the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[D_4]]></fr:tex> moment-map probes. Consistent with
the small-<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> record: the one genuine counterexample to a
naively-stated survivor identity in the whole program is over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {F}_q]]></fr:tex>
with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q = 2]]></fr:tex> at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 5]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0017/" title="Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0017/" display-uri="ffi-0017" type="local">Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0049/" title="The germ apparatus" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0049/" display-uri="ffi-0049" type="local">The germ apparatus</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: journal passes 6--8 (variational identities, thresholds,
anomaly law); distilled.tex Part 5.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0049/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0049</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0049/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The germ apparatus">The germ apparatus</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="avatar">3</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>A <html:em>pencil germ</html:em> is a lattice over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B = k\lbrack \lbrack  u \rbrack \rbrack \lbrack  u^{-1} \rbrack ]]></fr:tex>, a
polynomial-type ring in one variable over the formal power series ring
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[R = k\lbrack \lbrack  u \rbrack \rbrack ]]></fr:tex>. The <html:em>survivor algebra</html:em> of a germ carries
a cyclic cocycle; that cocycle is the germ-scale avatar of the trace
form.</html:p>
            <html:p>The <html:em>exponent dictionary</html:em> computes its graded pieces: the
dimension of the level-<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[m]]></fr:tex> piece is <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  r + \#\{\text {adjoint-torsion exponents of size} \ge  m\},
]]></fr:tex>
a consequence of the isomorphism <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {C}_{P} \cong  \mathcal {O}]]></fr:tex>
between the centralizer sheaf of the germ's pencil <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> and the
structure sheaf.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> This is avatar 3 — <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0013/" title="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/" display-uri="ffi-0013" type="local">the thesis</fr:link> made infinitesimal.
Avatar 2, the residue form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex>, has its <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[u]]></fr:tex>-adic
bottom here. Coisotropy of the cocycle is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0017/" title="Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0017/" display-uri="ffi-0017" type="local">Wall 1</fr:link>; the
attempt to upgrade it to a Lagrangian statement is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0013/" title="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/" display-uri="ffi-0013" type="local">The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 5; journal passes 8--11 (exponent
dictionary, component atlas).</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0013</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0013/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension">The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>principle</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Every bound the program needs is half of a nilpotent orbit dimension.
Nilpotent orbits carry the Kostant--Kirillov form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex> in every
characteristic; a subvariety of half dimension inside an orbit sits at
the Lagrangian bound, and dimension <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\le ]]></fr:tex> half is exactly what isotropy
forces.</html:p>
            <html:p>The thesis appears at three scales, and the three avatars of the
forest are its three instances.</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li><html:strong>Global</html:strong> — the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex> form on each orbit:
  <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link>, a theorem.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Semi-local</html:strong> — the residue form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex> on
  adjoint torsion at drop points: <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link>, a theorem.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Infinitesimal</html:strong> — the survivor cocycle at a pencil germ:
  <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>, a conjecture.</html:li></html:ul>
            <html:p>This is a principle, not a theorem: it is the reason the three
avatars have the same shape, and it is what tells you that
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link> is the right thing to be stuck on.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0012/" title="The three recurring numbers" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0012/" display-uri="ffi-0012" type="local">The three recurring numbers</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 2 (the avatar structure).</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0012/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0012</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0012/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The three recurring numbers">The three recurring numbers</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>lemma</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Fix <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lambda  \vdash  n]]></fr:tex> with conjugate <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lambda ']]></fr:tex>. Then
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  c(\lambda ) = \sum _{i,j} \min (\lambda _i, \lambda _j)
             = \sum _j (\lambda '_j)^2, \qquad 
  \dim  \mathcal {O}_{\lambda } = n^2 - c(\lambda ), \qquad 
  \dim  \mathrm {Fl}_{\lambda '} = \frac {1}{2} \dim  \mathcal {O}_{\lambda }.
]]></fr:tex></html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c(\lambda )]]></fr:tex> is the dimension of the centralizer of a
nilpotent of type <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lambda ]]></fr:tex>, in every characteristic: the centralizer
is the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k[T]]]></fr:tex>-module <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {Hom}]]></fr:tex> of the type modules, whose dimension is
the stated minimum-sum. For regular <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lambda ]]></fr:tex> the half is
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\frac {n(n - 1)}{2}]]></fr:tex> — the height-conjecture dimension, which is why
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">the height conjecture</fr:link> and <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link> are the same
statement at two scales.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: standard; journal sec:orbits.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0011</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0011/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The trace form">The trace form</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>The <html:em>trace form</html:em> on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathfrak {gl}_n]]></fr:tex>: <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \kappa (A, B) = \operatorname {tr}(AB),
]]></fr:tex>
perfect over every ring, symmetric, and invariant:
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa ([P, A], B) = -\kappa (A, [P, B])]]></fr:tex>. Twisted, it
identifies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathfrak {gl}_n]]></fr:tex> with its <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex>-twisted dual.</html:p>
            <html:p>Restricted to the commutator subspace of a fixed <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex>, its
alternation <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\kappa ([P, A], A) = 0]]></fr:tex> is the source of every
symplectic structure in the program. The Kostant--Kirillov form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex>
that makes <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">orbital varieties Lagrangian</fr:link>, the residue form
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex> of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link>, and the survivor cocycle of
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0017/" title="Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0017/" display-uri="ffi-0017" type="local">Wall 1</fr:link> are all restrictions of it.</html:p>
